## Current state

Every number below is rendered by `scripts/check_published_numbers.py`
from a live query against this repository's own database and
`evalset/questions.yaml`, never typed in by hand; see
`packages/api/src/groundwork_api/claims.py` for exactly how.

- Demo workspaces seeded: {{workspace_count}}
- Documents indexed: {{document_count}}
- Chunks stored: {{naive_chunk_count}} naive, {{structure_chunk_count}} structure aware
- Golden evaluation set: {{eval_question_count}} questions ({{eval_category_counts}})
- Retrieval evaluation harness: {{retrieval_metrics_status}}, both chunking strategies scored with and without reranking
- Prompt injection red team: {{injection_pass_count}} of {{injection_total_count}} passed
- Workspace isolation red team: {{workspace_isolation_pass_count}} of {{workspace_isolation_total_count}} passed
- Out of scope refusal: {{out_of_scope_pass_count}} of {{out_of_scope_total_count}} passed, a real and unflattering number, explained in full in RESULTS.md rather than tuned away

That last line is there on purpose. A relevance gate built for an
environment with no reachable embedding model or LLM key cannot reliably
tell an out-of-scope question from an in-scope one under a lexical
fallback, and RESULTS.md reports that plainly, with every failing case
shown, instead of adjusting a threshold until this one evaluation set
happens to score better.

## Architecture

Extract, chunk two ways, embed, index, retrieve, rerank, gate on
relevance, generate, then verify independently of the model that
answered: split each claim, check it against its cited chunk with a
model that never sees the generator's own reasoning, and confirm the
citation is real. See ARCHITECTURE.md for the full system design,
docs/chunking.md for the two chunking strategies compared head to head,
docs/faithfulness.md for how an answer is checked without trusting the
model that wrote it, and docs/security.md for the threat model,
including a real prompt injection defense measured against a planted
attack, not merely described.

## Stack

Python 3.12, FastAPI, SQLModel, Postgres with pgvector, sentence
transformers and a cross encoder reranker (both with deterministic
offline fallbacks), LiteLLM, Next.js 15 as a static export, TypeScript,
Docker, Fly.io, Neon, GitHub Actions, GitHub Pages.

